---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro';
import NextPage from '../../components/NextPage.astro';

// DRAFT (2026-11-24): noindex and out of the sitemap until the committee has reviewed it. Moved to /about/new-church 2026-11-27 and linked from the menu, home page and About pages.
// Written for someone with no church background: who we are first, belief in one sentence, then "why new"
// lightly, and the detail (Swedenborg's claims, Christian differences) further down for those who want it.
const doors = [
  {
    href: '#questions',
    eyebrow: 'New to all this',
    title: 'Life’s big questions',
    text: 'Why do bad things happen? What happens when we die? A few short, plain answers.',
  },
  {
    href: '#christian',
    eyebrow: 'From another church',
    title: 'What we share, and what is different',
    text: 'Same Jesus, same Bible, with a few ideas seen a little differently.',
  },
  {
    href: '/events/sunday-worship',
    eyebrow: 'Thinking of visiting',
    title: 'What a Sunday is like',
    text: 'Under an hour, a warm welcome, and tea afterwards.',
  },
];

const questions = [
  {
    q: 'What is life for?',
    a: 'We think it is about growing in love and being useful to other people. Happiness tends to turn up along the way, rather than as something we can chase directly.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do bad things happen?',
    a: 'We don’t believe God sends suffering, or that everything happens "for a reason". People are free, and freedom has to include the freedom to choose badly. God never wants anyone to suffer, and is always at work bringing healing and growth out of even the worst things.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What about forgiveness?',
    a: 'We understand God as love itself, so forgiveness is not something we have to beg for. It is already there. What is left for us is the harder part: putting things right, letting go of resentment, and taking the next step.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens when we die?',
    a: 'We believe life goes on, and that we carry on as ourselves, with the things we love. Heaven isn’t a prize handed out at the end. It is a way of life built on love and kindness, and we can start growing into it now.',
  },
];

const shared = [
  'The Bible is God’s Word',
  'Jesus Christ is Lord',
  'The Ten Commandments, and the two great commandments: love God and love your neighbour',
  'Prayer, worship and a life that puts faith into practice',
  'Life after death',
];

const different = [
  {
    title: 'One God, not three persons',
    text: 'The Lord Jesus Christ is the one God. We see Father, Son and Holy Spirit as soul, body and activity of that one person, the way a person has all three.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The Bible has a deeper meaning',
    text: 'Alongside the stories as they are written, we read the Bible for an inner meaning about our own spiritual lives. Most of what you will hear on a Sunday is about that.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Salvation is a journey',
    text: 'Being "born again" is not one moment but a lifetime of change and growth. We also don’t teach that the New Church is the only way to God. The Lord works through many churches.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Heaven and hell are chosen',
    text: 'God does not send anyone anywhere. People move towards the kind of life they have come to love, and heaven is open to everyone who loves God and tries to live well.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Marriage can last for ever',
    text: 'Love in marriage is a gift that can grow deeper and carry on after death, when it is built on shared spiritual values.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What is the New Church?"
  description="A small, friendly Christian church in Roseville. Who we are, what we believe in a sentence, and why it's called the New Church."
  currentPath="/about/new-church"
  noindex
>
  <main class="container-prose px-6 md:px-8 pt-10 md:pt-14 pb-16">
    <article class="prose-rnc">
      <div
        class="font-display font-semibold text-eyebrow tracking-eyebrow uppercase text-accent-primary mb-2.5 !mt-0"
      >
        ✦ New here?
      </div>
      <h1>What is the New Church?</h1>

      <p class="text-lg">
        The New Church is a Christian church: we follow Jesus Christ and read the
        Bible, and we try to live what we believe. People come to us for all
        sorts of reasons: a quiet hour on a Sunday, a chat over a cup of tea, or
        a place to think about the bigger questions in life. Some grew up going
        to church. Plenty didn’t.
      </p>

      <h2>What we believe, in a sentence</h2>
      <p>
        That Jesus Christ is the one God, and that God is love; that the Bible is
        sacred, with more to say about everyday life than it might first appear;
        and that faith is something you live, by turning away from what harms and
        caring for the people around you.
      </p>

      <h2>So why “new”?</h2>
      <p>
        It’s actually an old name. The New Church began in London in the 1780s,
        and has met here in Sydney since 1875. “New” was about looking at
        Christianity with fresh eyes: less about rules and fear, more about love
        and how we live.
      </p>
      <p>
        Many of those fresh ideas come from <strong>Emanuel Swedenborg</strong>,
        a Swedish scientist of the 1700s who spent the second half of his life
        writing about the Bible. The name comes from the “new Jerusalem” in the
        book of Revelation, which he read as a picture of a renewed Christianity
        rather than a new religion.
      </p>
      <p>
        You’ll hear his books read alongside the Bible on a Sunday, but you don’t
        need to have read any of them to feel at home. We don’t ask anyone to
        take his word for it, either: we read his work, ask questions and weigh
        it for ourselves, and you’re welcome to do the same.
      </p>
    </article>

    <section class="mt-12" aria-label="Where would you like to start?">
      <h2 class="text-h2 mt-0 mb-5">Where would you like to start?</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
        {
          doors.map((d) => (
            <Card variant="default" interactive href={d.href} eyebrow={d.eyebrow} title={d.title}>
              <p class="m-0 text-sm text-text-muted">{d.text}</p>
            </Card>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="questions" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-24 prose-rnc">
      <h2>Life’s big questions</h2>
      <p>
        You don’t need any background in church or the Bible. These are a few of
        the questions people bring to us, and how we tend to see them.
      </p>
      {
        questions.map((item) => (
          <>
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p>{item.a}</p>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </section>

    <section id="christian" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      <div class="prose-rnc">
        <h2 class="!mt-0">If you grew up in another Christian church</h2>
        <p>
          You will probably recognise most of what we say. The differences are
          mostly ones of emphasis. We aren’t asking you to leave anything behind,
          and we don’t claim to be better. Here is where we agree, and where we
          see things a little differently.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Card variant="tint" class="mt-6" title="What we share">
        <ul class="m-0 pl-5 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1.5 marker:text-accent-primary">
          {shared.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </Card>

      <h3 class="mt-8 mb-4 text-h3">Where we see things differently</h3>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-3">
        {
          different.map((d) => (
            <Card variant="default" title={d.title}>
              <p class="m-0 text-text-muted">{d.text}</p>
            </Card>
          ))
        }
      </div>

    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 prose-rnc">
      <h2 class="!mt-0">Come as you are</h2>
      <p>
        If you’d like to come along, just turn up. You don’t need to know anything, and nobody will put you on the
        spot. We meet in the brick church on Shirley Road, Roseville, on the first and third Sundays of the month at
        10am, with tea and coffee afterwards. Questions are always welcome, over tea or by email.
      </p>
    </section>

    <NextPage title="Our church" href="/about" label="Read about our church">
      Meet the people of Roseville New Church, our minister, and the story of the Society since 1875.
      <Fragment slot="more">
        <Button variant="secondary" href="/events/sunday-worship">Plan a Sunday visit</Button>
        <Button variant="ghost" href="/contact">Ask a question</Button>
      </Fragment>
    </NextPage>

    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-text-muted">
      Want to read more first? The New Church in Australia has a plain-language
      guide to its{' '}
      <a href="https://newchurch.net.au/beliefs" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">core beliefs</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
